@*
    A do-nothing wrapper whose only job is to be something ObservableForEach can put an @key on.

    Why it is needed: when ObservableForEach renders `@ChildContent(i)` directly in the loop, the
    compiler emits RenderTreeBuilder.AddContent(seq, RenderFragment<T>, value), and that overload
    wraps the fragment in a render-tree REGION. Blazor's keyed matching only matches frames that are
    direct siblings in the same frame list, so a @key the consumer writes inside ChildContent is
    trapped in that region and can never match an item across a positional shift.

    KeyedItem gives the loop a direct child of its own to key. It renders no element and no attribute
    of its own, so the consumer's markup is unchanged.

    It is only rendered when ObservableForEach.Key is set; with Key null the loop body is exactly
    what it always was.
*@
@ChildContent

@code {
    [Parameter] public RenderFragment? ChildContent { get; set; }
}
